// A diagnostic BELOW a multi-line string literal must name the line the mistake is on.
//
// This is the guard that did not exist, and its absence is why the bug survived: `DIAGNOSTIC_LINES`
// asserts an exact line for all ~615 xfail fixtures, and not one of them had a multi-line literal ahead
// of its error, so every row agreed with a lexer that could not count. The plain-string state was the
// only one in kama.l with no `{new_line}` rule — a raw newline was consumed as ordinary content by
// `single_string_char` and the line counter never moved — so every diagnostic after a literal fired that
// many lines EARLY, and the deficit accumulated over every literal in the file. The column stayed
// correct, which made it read as a compiler fault rather than as an offset.
//
// The first external project lost real time to it: a parse error in a file holding a WGSL shader named a
// line 27 above the mistake, and 27 was exactly the newline count of the shader string. Any file holding
// a shader is exposed by construction.
//
// ⚠️ The assertion here is the ROW in DIAGNOSTIC_LINES, not the message — a `.msg` alone would have
// passed throughout. Two literals, so the row also proves the deficit ACCUMULATES rather than being a
// one-off off-by-N.
fn string shaderA()
{
    return "
alpha
beta
gamma
";
}

fn string shaderB()
{
    return "
delta
epsilon
";
}

fn int32 main()
{
    int32 bad = "not a number";
    return bad;
}
